prompt:
You are a financial analyst revising a forecast based on a peer critique.

Original forecast: {
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "base_year": 2021,
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "forecast": [
    {
      "year": 2022,
      "revenue_cr": 191486,
      "net_income_cr": 38900,
      "eps": 104.0
    },
    {
      "year": 2023,
      "revenue_cr": 216679,
      "net_income_cr": 44200,
      "eps": 118.2
    },
    {
      "year": 2024,
      "revenue_cr": 240714,
      "net_income_cr": 49100,
      "eps": 131.3
    }
  ],
  "top_assumptions": [
    "Post-pandemic demand recovery drives constant-currency growth rebounding to ~7-8% in FY2022 and normalising toward the pre-pandemic 3-year CAGR of ~8-9% in INR terms, as Q1 FY2021 pandemic drag fully rolls off and digital transformation spending accelerates across BFSI, CMT, and Retail verticals.",
    "Net profit margin stabilises at ~20-21% of revenues (consistent with the FY2020-21 reported adjusted margin of ~20.3%), with the one-off legal claim provision of INR 1,218 crore from FY2021 not recurring, partially offset by continued employee cost pressure from wage hikes and headcount addition in a tight talent market."
  ],
  "reasoning": "FY2021 is anomalous: reported INR revenue growth was 4.6% but constant-currency growth was -0.8% due to the COVID-19 pandemic sharply depressing Q1 FY2021 revenues across all verticals; the prior year (FY2020) showed 7.2% INR growth and ~7% constant-currency growth. Accordingly, the latest year-on-year growth rate is not a reliable projection basis. The 3-5 year pre-pandemic CAGR for TCS was approximately 8-10% in INR terms; blending recovery mean-reversion (base effect from weak Q1 FY2021 lapping off) with this CAGR, a ~16-17% INR revenue growth is assumed for FY2022 (reflecting both real demand recovery and favourable base), moderating to ~13% in FY2023 and ~11% in FY2024 as growth normalises. The exceptional legal provision (INR 1,218 crore) is treated as non-recurring, supporting margin improvement in FY2022 and beyond. The balance sheet is debt-light with strong free cash flow generation (~INR 38,802 crore OCF in FY2021), capex modest at ~INR 2,719 crore, confirming no financial constraint on growth execution."
}
Critique: {
  "company": "TCS",
  "base_year": 2021,
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "assumption": "Post-pandemic demand recovery drives ~16-17% INR revenue growth in FY2022, moderating to ~13% in FY2023 and ~11% in FY2024, anchored by pre-pandemic CAGR mean-reversion and accelerating digital transformation spend across BFSI, CMT, and Retail verticals.",
      "failure_scenario": "A third COVID wave (Delta/Omicron variants) or a sharper-than-expected global macro slowdown in key markets like the US and Europe causes clients to freeze or defer large discretionary IT transformation programmes, resulting in growth falling materially short of the ~16-17% FY2022 target and compressing the entire three-year revenue trajectory by 300-500 bps per annum.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Net profit margin stabilises at ~20-21% of revenues, with the INR 1,218 crore legal provision treated as a non-recurring item and no structural deterioration from employee cost pressure.",
      "failure_scenario": "The Great Resignation-era talent war intensifies, forcing TCS into double-digit wage hikes and a sharp increase in subcontracting costs to manage attrition running above 15-17%; simultaneously, onsite delivery mix rises due to client demand for in-person engagement, inflating travel and facility costs, collectively compressing margins by 150-250 bps and invalidating the 20-21% margin floor assumption.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "The weak FY2021 base (COVID-depressed Q1) is treated as a one-time anomaly, and the pre-pandemic 3-5 year CAGR of ~8-10% in INR terms is used as the normalised long-run growth anchor for FY2023-FY2024.",
      "failure_scenario": "Structural shifts accelerated by the pandemic \u2014 such as permanent reduction in legacy application maintenance budgets, faster cloud commoditisation reducing services intensity, or intensifying competition from hyperscalers offering managed services \u2014 mean that the pre-pandemic CAGR is no longer a valid baseline, causing organic growth to settle structurally lower at 6-7% INR CAGR post-recovery, undermining the FY2023-FY2024 revenue projections.",
      "risk": "Medium"
    }
  ],
  "confidence_score": 52,
  "overall_critique": "The forecast's use of base-effect-driven mean reversion is methodologically sound given FY2021's anomalous nature, but the 16-17% FY2022 INR growth assumption is aggressive and highly sensitive to macro and talent-market risks that were clearly materialising at the time of the forecast. The margin stabilisation assumption, in particular, underweights the structural employee cost pressures that proved persistent well beyond FY2022, making the combined revenue and profitability targets difficult to achieve simultaneously."
}

Step 1: For each "High" risk assumption in the critique, adjust the corresponding forecast values by a conservative factor (reduce growth by 20-30% for that driver).
Step 2: For "Medium" risk assumptions, reduce growth by 10% for that driver.
Step 3: Leave "Low" risk assumptions unchanged.
Step 4: Calculate a confidence range: ± percentage based on the number of High (±15%) and Medium (±8%) risks.

RESPONSE FORMAT: Begin your response DIRECTLY with the opening brace { — no reasoning or text before the JSON.

Return ONLY valid JSON:
{
  "strategy": "<strategy>",
  "base_year": <YYYY>,
  "revised_forecast": [
    {"year": <YYYY>, "revenue_cr": <number>, "net_income_cr": <number>, "eps": <number>,
     "confidence_range_pct": <number>},
    ...
  ],
  "revisions_made": ["<description of each change>"],
  "final_confidence_score": <0-100>
}
